{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527869679,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxODg3NDk2"},"target":"bb03fbfe754ea44f994b9e151eef901d836da4d51fc185257fef5d10d8b04f42","message":"@kyleam Ran the following on a bunch of files in git. But they all end up being annexed as a result.\n\n```\ndatalad run bash -c 'for i in $(find . -name studyspec.json); do sed -i -e \"s/\\(bids_task.*\\)epi2dbold20iso[0-9]\\+/\\1orientation/g\" $i; done'\n```","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527869746,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxODg3OTAz"},"target":"bb03fbfe754ea44f994b9e151eef901d836da4d51fc185257fef5d10d8b04f42","message":"@kyleam Ran the following on a bunch of files in git. But they all end up being annexed as a result.\n\n```\ndatalad run bash -c 'for i in $(find . -name studyspec.json); do sed -i -e \"s/\\(bids_task.*\\)epi2dbold20iso[0-9]\\+/\\1orientation/g\" $i; done'\n```\n\nDo we need #2473?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1527876281,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5Mzk2MzMxNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2600#issuecomment-393963315"},"message":"\u003e Ran the following on a bunch of files in git. But they all end up being annexed as a result.\n\nThanks.  I agree that the current behavior is surprising/undesirable.\n\n\u003e Do we need #2473?\n\nI think it makes sense to add a `--to-git` flag to run (sorry for not yet responding to that issue), but I don't think it's a complete solution to this issue. If we have a `--to-git` option, that covers how the results are added on the initial run, but we would have to somehow propagate that information so that the rerun didn't annex them.  The main option I can think of is to check if the file is tracked in plain git and then decide whether to `git add` or `git annex add` it.  And that check alone would solve the issue here.\n\nBut I wonder whether this is something to be handled at the level of run.  Would it may make sense for `datalad add` call `git add` if a file's content is tracked in git?  In the (uncommon?) case where a user wants to move a regular file over to an annexed file, they could call `git annex add` directly.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1527876281,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDkxOTI5NDUz"},"target":"95c75c6cc8b393ff1a6144bdb4ad0eafd5d9ccc99cd62510548b6ffe4a4d4dca","message":"\u003e Ran the following on a bunch of files in git. But they all end up being annexed as a result.\n\nThanks.  I agree that the current behavior is surprising/undesirable.\n\n\u003e Do we need #2473?\n\nI think it makes sense to add a `--to-git` flag to run (sorry for not yet responding to that issue), but I don't think it's a complete solution to this issue. If we have a `--to-git` option, that covers how the results are added on the initial run, but we would have to somehow propagate that information so that a rerun didn't annex them.  The main option I can think of is to check if the file is tracked in plain git and then decide whether to `git add` or `git annex add` it.  And that check alone would solve the issue here.\n\nBut I wonder whether this is something to be handled at the level of run.  Would it may make sense for `datalad add` call `git add` if a file's content is tracked in git?  In the (uncommon?) case where a user wants to move a regular file over to an annexed file, they could call `git annex add` directly.","files":null}]}